From 14b2459262415ae93a970fc79ac224ab158fffdf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SteveDeFacto <5148995+SteveDeFacto@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:38:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Windows node: one share is one fraction of both CPU and the
 app RAM pool

The partition manager already gives a partition floor(NODE_APP_RAM_GB x cpuShare)
MiB, but the node advertised and admitted RAM as the pool less catalog floors: the
NucBox published 17% of its CPU beside 79.8 of 96 GB free while the manager held
57% of the pool. On the partition backend (sharesCoupled) the node now:

- frees ONE fraction: the owner reserve and cap bind RAM exactly as CPU, each
  deployment holds the larger of floor(pool x share) and what the manager's
  /vms says its partitions hold, and ramMbFree = floor(pool x cpuShareFree);
- reads the manager each pass and before a claim (refreshManagerView) and sells
  nothing new when that view is unread, malformed, stale, recovered, pool-less,
  or from another pool (capacity.ramUnknown; claimPolicy refuses), while work
  already here is never refused or given back for it;
- treats the version's RAM floor as a share requirement (effectiveCpuShare
  with the pool, whole percents up), so a floor raises the share or refuses;
- spawns at the share it counts (0xc13d4b6a was spawned at its 1% dial while
  the node counted 2%), adopts a partition sized for another share as it is
  (allocatedShare + reason on the record), and relaunches in place only when
  its owner pays for more than it holds and it fits, at most once per 10 min;
- rounds cpuShareFree down to millionths (0.16999999999999993 read as 16%).

isolation-client: GET /vms without a vms list is a protocol error, never an
empty inventory; views carry the pinned policy (the readiness witness only).
